<article class="env-lemma" id="0704">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0704">Lemma <span data-tag="0704">46.8.7</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Any $A$-module has a pure injective resolution. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Let $M \to N$ be a map of $A$-modules. Let $M \to M^\bullet $ be a universally exact resolution and let $N \to I^\bullet $ be a pure injective resolution. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(2)"><p>
There exists a map of complexes $M^\bullet \to I^\bullet $ inducing the given map 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  M = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(M^0 \to M^1) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(I^0 \to I^1) = N  \]
</div>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(3)"><p>
two maps $\alpha , \beta : M^\bullet \to I^\bullet $ inducing the same map $M \to N$ are homotopic. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This lemma is dual to Lemma <a href="/tag/0703" data-tag="0703">46.8.6</a>. The proof is identical, except one has to reverse all the arrows. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>